1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,760
During World War II, American tank crews duked it out with Nazi panzers in a high explosive duel to the death.

2
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:18,060
Crammed inside 30 tons of rolling steel, the GIs went up against a better armed and more experienced enemy.

3
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:22,740
All too often, the tanks became a steel coffin.

4
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:27,920
It was a war where guts and courage were the secret to survival.

5
00:00:27,921 --> 00:00:32,920
Meet the tank crews next on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:45,671 --> 00:00:53,780
The tank that put the teeth into an American armored division in World War II was known officially as medium tank M4.

7
00:00:53,781 --> 00:01:01,600
But to the five GIs that manned each of these steel monsters, it's better remembered as the General Sherman.

8
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:17,820
The crews hoped that their Sherman would live up to its Civil War namesake, cut a path through hostile territory, and leave in its wake a defeated enemy.

9
00:01:17,821 --> 00:01:31,580
The Sherman was conceived not too many years earlier, before America was even at war, as a weapon intended to help the infantry out of a tight spot.

10
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:41,560
It was supposed to roll in and blast away at machine gun nests, enemy troops and other soft targets.

11
00:01:41,561 --> 00:01:48,800
but on the battlefields of europe the sherman was forced to take on a much deadlier job

12
00:01:48,801 --> 00:01:53,260
shooting it out point blank with enemy tanks

13
00:01:53,261 --> 00:02:07,261
everybody was frightened everybody was scared but it got built into you you know this is what

14
00:02:09,640 --> 00:02:13,420
you were going to do and you're going to see it through one way or another.

15
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:17,400
What are you going to do?

16
00:02:17,401 --> 00:02:21,660
Are you going to repel and try and just refuse to get back in? You just

17
00:02:21,661 --> 00:02:22,725
didn't do it.

18
00:02:22,726 --> 00:02:28,540
Every day you would just say, man, if I can just make it through today,

19
00:02:28,541 --> 00:02:32,798
I got it made. But that came up every day.

20
00:02:32,799 --> 00:02:37,360
Every time we got into a tank, it was suicide

21
00:02:37,361 --> 00:02:43,200
in a sense that we could have been killed at any time and we knew it.

22
00:02:44,140 --> 00:02:49,340
But at the same time, I think we all felt that we were still somewhat invincible.

23
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:57,260
During the war, the American Army fielded 16 different armored divisions.

24
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:01,360
They were outfitted with a mix of two types of tanks.

25
00:03:01,361 --> 00:03:07,400
a small tank named the General Stewart and the heavier Sherman tank.

26
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:15,960
Only two of those 16 divisions, the second and the third, were designated as heavy armored divisions,

27
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:20,720
meaning that their regiments were equipped primarily with Shermans.

28
00:03:22,960 --> 00:03:26,060
A heavy armored division was made up of three regiments,

29
00:03:26,061 --> 00:03:31,480
two armored regiments and a regiment of mechanized infantry,

30
00:03:32,940 --> 00:03:38,100
plus field artillery, medical staff, supply people, signals people,

31
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:42,900
reconnaissance units, combat engineers, and a maintenance battalion.

32
00:03:43,540 --> 00:03:45,900
Altogether, nearly 14,000 men.

33
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:51,820
Placed bumper to bumper, their vehicles would stretch for almost 10 miles.

34
00:03:51,821 --> 00:04:01,980
but a heavy armored division's combat power its reason for being with its 232 Sherman tanks

35
00:04:01,981 --> 00:04:08,640
the third armored division had been formed back in the spring of 1941

36
00:04:08,641 --> 00:04:16,240
in camps across the United States the men spent almost two years learning the technology and the

37
00:04:16,241 --> 00:04:21,180
tactics of armored warfare and how to work together as a team.

38
00:04:23,140 --> 00:04:29,140
Once in England, they trained for 10 more months and prepared themselves for the invasion of Europe.

39
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:42,800
They came ashore at Omaha Beach in Normandy, France in late June 1944, three weeks after D-Day.

40
00:04:42,801 --> 00:04:50,720
And on the morning of June 29th, the men of the 3rd Armored Division got their baptism of fire.

41
00:04:52,100 --> 00:04:58,260
They attacked the Germans dug in around Villiers-Foussard, a small village in the French hedgerow country.

42
00:04:59,980 --> 00:05:12,020
In the dense tangle of trees and narrow roads, the American tankers came face to face with the big German tanks with guns described by 1GI the size of telephone poles.

43
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:23,020
The German Mark IV and the 53-ton Mark V Panther tanks,

44
00:05:23,560 --> 00:05:26,640
with their high-velocity 75-millimeter guns,

45
00:05:26,780 --> 00:05:28,260
made mincemeat of the Shermans.

46
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:35,820
The German armor-piercing shells punched through one side of the Sherman

47
00:05:35,821 --> 00:05:37,240
and straight out the other.

48
00:05:39,020 --> 00:05:42,720
In the maelstrom of close combat, tanks were quickly knocked out.

49
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:49,800
blown to pieces or set ablaze incinerating the trapped men.

50
00:05:49,801 --> 00:05:56,800
An intense artillery barrage forced the German defenders into retreat and the Americans won the battle.

51
00:05:56,801 --> 00:06:03,800
But the tankers had suffered a terrific beating.

52
00:06:03,801 --> 00:06:11,800
Belton Cooper was a 25-year-old ordnance officer in the 3rd Armored Division, tasked with recovering and replacing the battle-damaged tanks.

53
00:06:12,800 --> 00:06:17,620
All morning long, they start bringing in these tanks

54
00:06:17,621 --> 00:06:19,280
with these broken and twisted bodies inside,

55
00:06:19,420 --> 00:06:21,200
and I was absolutely devastated.

56
00:06:21,420 --> 00:06:24,300
I thought, Cooper, how in the hell are we going to win this war?

57
00:06:24,360 --> 00:06:25,880
The Germans are shooting the hell out of these tanks.

58
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:29,380
I mean, that's the first time I seriously doubt if we could win this war.

59
00:06:29,460 --> 00:06:30,740
The Germans were just devastating us.

60
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:32,720
They were just slaughters right and left.

61
00:06:36,260 --> 00:06:38,160
At the end of its first day of the war,

62
00:06:38,161 --> 00:06:43,080
the 3rd Armored Division had lost 351 men and 31 tanks.

63
00:06:46,020 --> 00:06:48,500
The relentless power of the German tanks

64
00:06:48,501 --> 00:06:50,440
had caught the American crews off guard.

65
00:06:51,780 --> 00:06:55,600
They were clearly outgunned by the panzers of Hitler's Third Reich.

66
00:06:56,700 --> 00:06:59,860
The American tankers had learned an unexpected lesson.

67
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:03,600
Their Sherman was not the best tank,

68
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:05,400
as they had been led to believe.

69
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:16,100
At 69 tons, the German King Tiger tank was nearly twice the size of the Allies' main battle tank, the Sherman.

70
00:07:16,700 --> 00:07:18,480
Tank crews will return in a moment.

71
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:25,480
The first confrontation with Nazi tanks in Normandy, France in June 1944

72
00:07:25,481 --> 00:07:29,380
proved to the tankers of the American Armored Force

73
00:07:29,381 --> 00:07:33,420
that their M4 Sherman was no match for the enemy tanks.

74
00:07:33,421 --> 00:07:38,080
too many Shermans and too many trained crewmen

75
00:07:38,081 --> 00:07:39,700
had been lost in the fight

76
00:07:39,701 --> 00:07:45,160
it was really a policy that was adopted

77
00:07:45,161 --> 00:07:48,060
at the beginning of the war that caused the

78
00:07:48,061 --> 00:07:50,580
loss of these soldiers

79
00:07:50,581 --> 00:07:54,960
it was a matter of the American government

80
00:07:54,961 --> 00:07:57,520
chose production over research and development

81
00:07:57,521 --> 00:08:00,620
and made production paramount

82
00:08:00,621 --> 00:08:08,680
research and development of new tanks became a low priority for the u.s army at the end of world war one

83
00:08:08,681 --> 00:08:16,840
in 1920 congress passed the national defense act which reorganized the post-war army

84
00:08:16,841 --> 00:08:22,800
and abolished the fledgling tank corps innovation virtually came to a standstill

85
00:08:22,801 --> 00:08:29,800
the only new ideas came from an eccentric inventor named walter christie

86
00:08:29,801 --> 00:08:37,920
In this 1931 film, a radical Christie design is put through its paces for the Army brass.

87
00:08:39,940 --> 00:08:46,560
Christie's secret was a unique suspension that transformed the tank into something like a sports car with a cannon.

88
00:08:48,760 --> 00:08:52,160
But the Army's response was less than enthusiastic.

89
00:08:52,920 --> 00:08:59,580
The Army had a lot of problem dealing with him because he was eccentric and he insisted on everything being his way.

90
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:01,920
and the Army couldn't modify or change it.

91
00:09:02,380 --> 00:09:04,380
And eventually they just gave up dealing with him.

92
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:08,820
Frustrated by the Army's indifference,

93
00:09:09,420 --> 00:09:10,960
Christie took his ideas to Europe.

94
00:09:12,380 --> 00:09:14,760
Ironically, many of the German tanks

95
00:09:14,761 --> 00:09:17,500
that the U.S. Army would come up against in World War II

96
00:09:17,501 --> 00:09:20,260
sported a Christie-style suspension.

97
00:09:24,300 --> 00:09:26,760
Left without a tank corps to supervise

98
00:09:26,761 --> 00:09:28,800
the development of armored fighting vehicles,

99
00:09:28,801 --> 00:09:34,880
the infantry and the cavalry branches designed their own tanks

100
00:09:38,880 --> 00:09:43,040
the infantry looked at tanks as weapons that should support riflemen on the

101
00:09:43,041 --> 00:09:50,360
assault they needed to be maneuverable and pack a lot of firepower

102
00:09:50,361 --> 00:09:58,240
in 1939 the infantry settled on a tank called the M2 medium it had a crew of

103
00:09:58,241 --> 00:10:04,600
six no less than eight 30 caliber machine guns and a short barrel 37

104
00:10:04,601 --> 00:10:08,700
millimeter cannon in a top turret

105
00:10:09,300 --> 00:10:13,620
the cavalry on the other hand had considered its traditional roles in

106
00:10:13,621 --> 00:10:20,180
scouting and pursuit and put its money in lighter faster tanks

107
00:10:20,460 --> 00:10:25,920
they were armed with either machine guns or in later models a 37 millimeter

108
00:10:25,921 --> 00:10:36,080
cannon the light tanks required just four crewmen harley swenson drove a light tank at the time we

109
00:10:36,081 --> 00:10:42,960
had just shoe signals on our shoulders and stuff you know you didn't have a radio contact it was

110
00:10:42,961 --> 00:10:49,920
mostly manual contacts for driving and backing up right or left when they wanted you to stop

111
00:10:49,921 --> 00:10:51,960
they'd give you one good rap, you know.

112
00:10:52,320 --> 00:10:53,580
You know you were supposed to stop.

113
00:10:57,580 --> 00:10:59,800
On September 1st, 1939,

114
00:11:00,420 --> 00:11:02,000
the Germans surprised the world

115
00:11:02,001 --> 00:11:04,460
with the power of their blitzkrieg attack on Poland.

116
00:11:06,600 --> 00:11:07,700
Compared to the Germans,

117
00:11:08,340 --> 00:11:11,100
the American tanks back home looked like toys.

118
00:11:14,980 --> 00:11:16,880
On July 10th, 1940,

119
00:11:16,881 --> 00:11:19,240
the Army created the Armored Force,

120
00:11:19,920 --> 00:11:21,920
and immediately began to play catch up.

121
00:11:23,160 --> 00:11:26,260
Army planners needed a new tank, and fast.

122
00:11:27,760 --> 00:11:32,920
Tank builders at Chrysler wanted to replace the small 37 millimeter of the M2 medium

123
00:11:33,420 --> 00:11:37,320
with a larger 75 millimeter cannon already in the inventory.

124
00:11:44,180 --> 00:11:48,160
But they hadn't yet figured out how to mount that big gun in a rotating turret.

125
00:11:49,920 --> 00:11:58,200
So they kept the 37-millimeter gun in the top turret and mounted the larger 75 in a sponson on the side of the tank.

126
00:12:03,100 --> 00:12:12,880
Called the M3 medium, the new tank made its spectacular public debut in April 1941 at Chrysler's giant Detroit tank arsenal.

127
00:12:19,920 --> 00:12:26,670
The M3 went to war with the British and U.S.

128
00:12:26,671 --> 00:12:33,420
armies fighting the Germans in North Africa.

129
00:12:35,480 --> 00:12:41,480
They found that since the 75mm gun had a very limited traverse, it was difficult to aim.

130
00:12:42,760 --> 00:12:46,260
The whole vehicle had to be moved to align the gun on a target.

131
00:12:46,960 --> 00:12:49,460
And that cost precious time in the heat of battle.

132
00:12:49,920 --> 00:12:54,080
But the M3 was only a stopgap.

133
00:12:55,000 --> 00:13:02,880
Before it was even in combat, the Army Ordnance Department was working on a new tank with a 75mm gun in a top turret.

134
00:13:04,160 --> 00:13:07,960
A turret that could traverse 360 degrees.

135
00:13:09,340 --> 00:13:15,520
In October 1941, this design was standardized as medium tank M4.

136
00:13:16,040 --> 00:13:18,760
And would become known as the Sherman.

137
00:13:19,920 --> 00:13:26,920
The first Sherman rolled off the assembly line in February 1942.

138
00:13:26,921 --> 00:13:33,920
Soon, tanks would be coming off the line at a rate of 2,000 a month.

139
00:13:33,921 --> 00:13:38,920
Chrysler's Detroit tank arsenal was the major builder of the tank,

140
00:13:38,921 --> 00:13:42,920
but other manufacturers like the American Locomotive Company,

141
00:13:42,921 --> 00:13:47,920
Ford, and the Pullman Car Company also built Shermans.

142
00:13:47,921 --> 00:13:50,220
The Sherman had a five-man crew.

143
00:13:50,840 --> 00:13:53,760
The driver and an assistant driver worked in the hull.

144
00:13:54,800 --> 00:13:58,900
The loader, the gunner, and the commander worked in the turret.

145
00:14:00,140 --> 00:14:03,900
For them, the tank's real beauty was its simplicity.

146
00:14:05,220 --> 00:14:12,420
The Sherman tank was manned by farm boys that had been used to working with tractors and that sort of thing.

147
00:14:12,421 --> 00:14:21,760
When they sat down in the seat where we are right now, they weren't at a loss because mechanics had been a major part of their life.

148
00:14:22,780 --> 00:14:25,140
The problem in a tank is this.

149
00:14:25,520 --> 00:14:32,540
There's so much equipment jammed inside of this hull that what you find in it is a super tight, confined quarters.

150
00:14:32,820 --> 00:14:37,000
They get very hot and very dirty very fast.

151
00:14:37,001 --> 00:14:42,580
And at full power, particularly if this tank was buttoned up, that is the hatches down,

152
00:14:43,200 --> 00:14:47,060
the noise would be so much that you could barely hear yourself scream.

153
00:14:49,200 --> 00:14:56,280
The first Shermans out of the factory were shipped overseas to outfit British armored units fighting the Germans in North Africa.

154
00:14:58,200 --> 00:15:06,540
In their first combat in September 1942, the British knocked out some enemy tanks and lost a couple of Shermans.

155
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,560
The fight ended more or less in a draw.

156
00:15:12,280 --> 00:15:18,400
The first Shermans in U.S. hands met the enemy in Tunisia on December 6, 1942.

157
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:26,740
A company of five tanks from the 2nd Armored Division attacked German tanks and anti-tank gun positions.

158
00:15:28,320 --> 00:15:30,820
All five Shermans were destroyed.

159
00:15:30,821 --> 00:15:37,340
by the time the U.S. Army was fighting the Germans in Sicily and Italy

160
00:15:37,341 --> 00:15:41,260
it was clear the M4 Sherman was outclassed

161
00:15:41,980 --> 00:15:46,280
its 75mm cannon had a low muzzle velocity

162
00:15:46,281 --> 00:15:50,380
the speed at which the shell left the barrel was too slow

163
00:15:50,381 --> 00:15:52,560
to punch a hole in the thick German armor

164
00:15:52,561 --> 00:15:59,800
the men shared stories of watching their shells harmlessly bounce off Nazi tanks

165
00:15:59,801 --> 00:16:04,900
The German guns, however, with their higher muzzle velocity

166
00:16:04,901 --> 00:16:07,280
Easily riddled the Shermans

167
00:16:07,281 --> 00:16:11,020
With a weak gun and thin armor

168
00:16:11,021 --> 00:16:13,640
The Sherman was a death trap

169
00:16:13,641 --> 00:16:21,620
Inventor Walter Christie spent five years developing the suspended cannon tank

170
00:16:21,621 --> 00:16:25,260
At an unprecedented cost of $382,000

171
00:16:25,261 --> 00:16:28,040
Tank crews will return in a moment

172
00:16:28,041 --> 00:16:32,156
The unavoidable compromise that builders of the U.S.

173
00:16:32,157 --> 00:16:37,300
M4 Sherman had to make between firepower, protection and mobility

174
00:16:37,301 --> 00:16:42,540
would become even more obvious as the Allied forces came ashore in Normandy, France.

175
00:16:43,540 --> 00:16:48,020
During the summer of 1944, the Sherman crews would suffer considerably.

176
00:16:48,021 --> 00:17:00,920
The French farm country, just inland from the D-Day invasion beaches, was a patchwork

177
00:17:00,921 --> 00:17:07,940
of small fields bordered by dense mounds of dirt, roots and trees.

178
00:17:07,941 --> 00:17:14,880
These so-called hedgerows made perfect hiding places for the German defenders.

179
00:17:14,881 --> 00:17:18,120
Their weapon of choice was the Panzerfaust.

180
00:17:18,121 --> 00:17:22,640
This German invention fired a warhead called a shaped charge.

181
00:17:22,641 --> 00:17:28,500
When it hit, the energy of the explosion would focus to a point and the hot jet would melt

182
00:17:28,501 --> 00:17:35,200
through the armor in a split second.

183
00:17:35,201 --> 00:17:44,200
In this German training film, the weapon penetrates nearly five inches of steel, twice the thickness

184
00:17:44,201 --> 00:17:45,600
of the Sherman's frontal armor.

185
00:17:47,260 --> 00:17:49,860
You wouldn't think with a little hole as small as that

186
00:17:49,861 --> 00:17:51,660
to where it went through that it could do all the damage

187
00:17:51,661 --> 00:17:52,860
it did inside of a tank.

188
00:17:53,300 --> 00:17:56,000
But when it blew out inside, it just tore up everything.

189
00:17:59,460 --> 00:18:01,120
The Panzerfaust was similar,

190
00:18:01,380 --> 00:18:03,780
but far superior to the American bazooka.

191
00:18:04,820 --> 00:18:06,880
It didn't have the range of the bazooka,

192
00:18:07,220 --> 00:18:09,240
but that didn't matter in the hedgerow country.

193
00:18:14,200 --> 00:18:25,560
But the most lethal anti-tank weapons in Normandy were the German Panther tanks with their high-velocity 75-millimeter guns and the bigger Tiger tanks with 88s.

194
00:18:29,720 --> 00:18:35,300
July 11, 1944, was one of the most critical days in the Battle of Normandy.

195
00:18:36,640 --> 00:18:40,600
The Germans launched a massive counterattack near the town of St. Lowe.

196
00:18:40,601 --> 00:18:47,040
Sherman tanks from the 3rd Armored Division moved forward to repel the onslaught

197
00:18:47,041 --> 00:18:53,840
The American tankers soon found themselves overwhelmed

198
00:18:53,841 --> 00:18:57,920
Walter Stitt was 20 years old and a gunner

199
00:18:57,921 --> 00:19:01,640
A lieutenant came over and ordered the tank commander to get out of the tank

200
00:19:01,641 --> 00:19:02,840
Said he was going to take over

201
00:19:02,841 --> 00:19:07,260
And found out later the lieutenant had already lost two tanks that day

202
00:19:07,261 --> 00:19:08,620
And this was his third tank

203
00:19:08,621 --> 00:19:16,200
and so we got in and we were sitting still and uh all of a sudden the platoon sergeant started

204
00:19:16,201 --> 00:19:21,680
yelling over the phone said lieutenant they're shooting at you back up back up then the the

205
00:19:21,681 --> 00:19:27,260
sergeant got on him again said lieutenant back up back up and i looked and he he just was paralyzed

206
00:19:27,261 --> 00:19:34,140
and he reached over and he grabbed his mic button and pushed the button and said driver back up

207
00:19:34,141 --> 00:19:38,360
bang just like that was hit and the shell hit right in front of the gunner and killed the gunner

208
00:19:38,361 --> 00:19:39,420
and the tank commander both.

209
00:19:40,320 --> 00:19:42,280
Of course, then survival gets in,

210
00:19:42,320 --> 00:19:43,480
you said, I got to get out of here

211
00:19:43,481 --> 00:19:44,540
and get out of here fast

212
00:19:44,541 --> 00:19:45,780
because if they hit me once,

213
00:19:45,840 --> 00:19:46,560
they'll get me again.

214
00:19:49,180 --> 00:19:52,000
Chuck Miller was also 20 and a loader.

215
00:19:52,940 --> 00:19:55,700
This, too, was his first experience in combat.

216
00:19:56,680 --> 00:19:58,500
His commander and gunner were killed

217
00:19:58,501 --> 00:20:00,600
by a direct hit almost immediately.

218
00:20:01,380 --> 00:20:03,960
You shouldn't, but you just lose it all there

219
00:20:03,961 --> 00:20:05,820
for a minute there.

220
00:20:05,900 --> 00:20:07,160
You just don't know what's going on.

221
00:20:07,161 --> 00:20:09,980
and all you can think about is getting out of the tank.

222
00:20:14,580 --> 00:20:19,740
By July 16th, reinforcements had helped repel the German offensive at St. Lowe.

223
00:20:21,280 --> 00:20:24,760
By then, the division had lost 87 tanks.

224
00:20:27,720 --> 00:20:28,960
Bert Close was 19.

225
00:20:29,620 --> 00:20:32,620
His tank company lost 12 of its 16 tanks.

226
00:20:32,621 --> 00:20:44,440
The strategy of sending tanks up roads one behind the other where there was no maneuverability was terrible.

227
00:20:45,580 --> 00:20:48,880
One way or the other, they were just sitting ducks.

228
00:20:51,600 --> 00:20:55,120
23-year-old Clement Elizondo was a gunner.

229
00:20:56,540 --> 00:21:00,060
There were several bad mistakes made.

230
00:21:00,061 --> 00:21:06,520
One, we went in with no infantry

231
00:21:06,521 --> 00:21:09,600
We had plenty of infantry units around

232
00:21:09,601 --> 00:21:12,780
So I don't know what happened

233
00:21:12,781 --> 00:21:15,320
But it was a pretty bad mistake

234
00:21:15,321 --> 00:21:20,280
I was totally disgusted both with the Sherman tank

235
00:21:20,281 --> 00:21:24,920
And with the people who were making the decisions on how to use them

236
00:21:24,921 --> 00:21:28,440
I thought, what kind of fools do they take us for?

237
00:21:30,060 --> 00:21:37,320
The army had never before experienced this kind of warfare in this kind of terrain.

238
00:21:38,240 --> 00:21:42,800
Every day in the hedgerows, the armored warriors learned a costly lesson.

239
00:21:43,540 --> 00:21:49,580
If they could break out of Normandy and the tangle of hedgerows, they would hit flat, open country.

240
00:21:50,240 --> 00:21:52,420
Better country for maneuvering tanks.

241
00:21:53,660 --> 00:21:57,540
They tried by fitting a handful of Shermans with bulldozer blades.

242
00:21:57,541 --> 00:22:02,140
the tank dozer would knock down the hedgerow

243
00:22:02,141 --> 00:22:04,820
and other tanks would rush into the breach

244
00:22:04,821 --> 00:22:08,260
but this had a serious drawback

245
00:22:08,261 --> 00:22:12,720
the Germans soon learned that wherever the tank dozer was

246
00:22:12,721 --> 00:22:15,280
that was where the breakthrough was going to happen

247
00:22:15,281 --> 00:22:19,360
the Germans then concentrated their fire at those points

248
00:22:19,361 --> 00:22:21,260
with murderous effect

249
00:22:21,261 --> 00:22:31,880
A sergeant named Curtis Cullen of the 102nd Cavalry Reconnaissance Squadron came up with

250
00:22:31,881 --> 00:22:39,500
a remarkable solution. He took scrap iron girders and welded them to the front of a

251
00:22:39,501 --> 00:22:40,717
tank's hull.

252
00:22:40,718 --> 00:22:48,320
On July 22nd, a tank commanded by 31-year-old Albert Genest was fitted with

253
00:22:48,321 --> 00:22:52,360
Cullen's iron tusks for a secret demonstration for the Army brass.

254
00:22:54,280 --> 00:23:03,340
I was set up there with my crew, and we get in position, and we got the order to hit it.

255
00:23:04,340 --> 00:23:12,360
And we hit it, and we went through, and we went back to the bivouac about how long it lasted.

256
00:23:12,361 --> 00:23:22,560
Cullen's device called the hedge chopper or the rhino became an instant hit the third armored

257
00:23:22,561 --> 00:23:28,860
division commander wanted 57 of his tanks fitted with the hedge choppers by the next morning the

258
00:23:28,861 --> 00:23:34,860
task of mass producing the device fell to the division maintenance crews well they had to make

259
00:23:34,861 --> 00:23:38,940
a different hedge chopper of each tank because the parts were all different we just cut up parts and

260
00:23:38,941 --> 00:23:40,740
and tried to put them together in the way we could.

261
00:23:40,741 --> 00:23:42,900
So the individual men had to improvise

262
00:23:42,901 --> 00:23:44,560
and design their own hedge choppers.

263
00:23:44,561 --> 00:23:46,460
We'd have one guy cutting up the steel,

264
00:23:46,461 --> 00:23:48,460
one crew was fitting the steel together,

265
00:23:48,461 --> 00:23:50,180
another crew was coming well enough.

266
00:23:52,620 --> 00:23:54,560
Maintenance men worked around the clock

267
00:23:54,561 --> 00:23:56,460
cutting and fitting the hedge choppers.

268
00:23:59,120 --> 00:24:01,860
They were racing to rig as many tanks as possible

269
00:24:01,861 --> 00:24:03,620
in time for Operation Cobra.

270
00:24:04,680 --> 00:24:06,120
The upcoming Allied attempt

271
00:24:06,121 --> 00:24:07,800
to break through the German lines.

272
00:24:08,940 --> 00:24:11,880
The big attack was scheduled to begin the next morning.

273
00:24:14,440 --> 00:24:19,520
We found out it took 40-man hours of labor to weld up a hedge chopper.

274
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,740
And we only had 40 men. We had 7 hours to go.

275
00:24:23,400 --> 00:24:25,400
So we worked all night long, worked our butts off.

276
00:24:25,860 --> 00:24:29,460
But instead of 57 hedge choppers, we had 7 the next morning.

277
00:24:29,920 --> 00:24:30,880
That's all we had.

278
00:24:31,540 --> 00:24:34,240
But fortunately, the good Lord looked after us.

279
00:24:34,241 --> 00:24:38,480
and the storms and the rain and the weather was so bad

280
00:24:38,481 --> 00:24:40,800
that the Air Force had delayed the attack for three days.

281
00:24:41,400 --> 00:24:44,120
Well, in three days, we got about half of them on there ready to go.

282
00:24:44,200 --> 00:24:47,440
We never had the 57, but we got about half of them, about 25 or so.

283
00:24:48,360 --> 00:24:50,640
And that was the thing that really saved the day.

284
00:24:54,920 --> 00:24:57,600
After the weather cleared on July 25th,

285
00:24:57,601 --> 00:25:02,600
a combination of air power, artillery, infantry, and armor

286
00:25:02,601 --> 00:25:04,240
broke the German lines.

287
00:25:05,580 --> 00:25:06,960
Enemy containment collapsed

288
00:25:06,961 --> 00:25:10,400
and the Allied armies hurtled into the heart of France.

289
00:25:12,660 --> 00:25:16,260
The 3rd Armored Division moved 17 miles in two days

290
00:25:16,261 --> 00:25:18,460
and lost only two tanks.

291
00:25:20,440 --> 00:25:23,620
It was the best single innovation that I know of

292
00:25:23,621 --> 00:25:25,740
that improved the capability of the Sherman tank.

293
00:25:26,060 --> 00:25:27,500
And in fact, I think without the headshot,

294
00:25:27,580 --> 00:25:28,580
I don't think we'd have gotten out of nowhere.

295
00:25:28,660 --> 00:25:29,940
I think we'd have been bogged down there.

296
00:25:29,960 --> 00:25:31,420
I don't think we could have really broken out.

297
00:25:32,600 --> 00:25:38,400
For his invention of the hedge chopper, Sergeant Cullen received the Legion of Merit.

298
00:25:39,320 --> 00:25:42,820
And the Sherman crews got a new lease on life.

299
00:25:46,600 --> 00:25:52,204
More than 49,000 Sherman tanks, each weighing 35 tons, were built by the U.S.

300
00:25:52,205 --> 00:25:53,660
during World War II.

301
00:25:54,320 --> 00:25:56,020
Tank crews will return in a moment.

302
00:25:56,021 --> 00:26:04,860
It was during the pitch battles to break free of the French hedgerows

303
00:26:04,861 --> 00:26:09,520
that the crews of the American M4 Sherman discovered its greatest deficiencies.

304
00:26:10,880 --> 00:26:13,740
The 75-millimeter gun was too weak,

305
00:26:14,520 --> 00:26:18,060
and at just two and a half inches, the armor was too thin.

306
00:26:20,420 --> 00:26:23,100
The Sherman's hull was penetrated so easily

307
00:26:23,101 --> 00:26:27,680
that both the enemy and the crews themselves took to calling the tank a Ronson,

308
00:26:28,840 --> 00:26:31,580
an allusion to the American cigarette lighter

309
00:26:31,581 --> 00:26:35,600
that was advertised as always lighting the first time.

310
00:26:40,140 --> 00:26:42,840
The tankers often added their own armor.

311
00:26:43,640 --> 00:26:48,480
Steel plates, called applique armor, were welded over vulnerable spots.

312
00:26:48,481 --> 00:26:51,040
spare steel tracks

313
00:26:51,041 --> 00:26:52,620
sandbags

314
00:26:52,621 --> 00:26:55,120
and even logs were slung on the tank

315
00:26:55,121 --> 00:26:57,860
in an attempt to break up the searing blast

316
00:26:57,861 --> 00:26:59,020
of the Panzerfaust

317
00:26:59,021 --> 00:27:03,460
by the summer of 1944

318
00:27:03,461 --> 00:27:05,660
improved Shermans had begun

319
00:27:05,661 --> 00:27:06,860
to arrive in quantity

320
00:27:06,861 --> 00:27:10,180
upgraded Shermans

321
00:27:10,181 --> 00:27:12,740
like the M4A3E8

322
00:27:12,741 --> 00:27:14,260
or EZ8

323
00:27:14,261 --> 00:27:16,300
with its thicker armor

324
00:27:16,301 --> 00:27:17,980
better suspension

325
00:27:17,981 --> 00:27:21,940
and a safer way to store ammunition was a welcomed addition.

326
00:27:22,920 --> 00:27:27,940
But the most appreciated improvement was the new 76-millimeter gun.

327
00:27:30,180 --> 00:27:33,920
Although the shell was just a scant millimeter wider than the 75,

328
00:27:34,560 --> 00:27:38,900
the propellant capacity of the casing was about 50% greater.

329
00:27:42,300 --> 00:27:45,160
That gave the shell a much higher muzzle velocity

330
00:27:45,161 --> 00:27:47,960
and a better chance of penetrating German armor.

331
00:27:47,980 --> 00:27:56,840
But the armored warriors had improved their chances of survival not by just improving the tank.

332
00:27:57,420 --> 00:28:00,700
They had also improved the way they used the tank.

333
00:28:04,980 --> 00:28:11,300
Tankers learned to attack with the support of not just infantry, but with artillery and air power.

334
00:28:12,440 --> 00:28:17,380
This new way of fighting as a team came to be called combined arms warfare.

335
00:28:17,980 --> 00:28:26,000
Oftentimes, aviators from the Army Air Corps traveled with the tanks to help coordinate the attack.

336
00:28:27,380 --> 00:28:30,860
We had a liaison assigned to each company from the Air Corps,

337
00:28:31,460 --> 00:28:36,040
and they had a special radio where they could talk to their pilots.

338
00:28:37,020 --> 00:28:43,820
And he rode right along with us and communicated with airplanes when we were getting their support.

339
00:28:43,821 --> 00:28:50,400
and it was really good when you could when you'd see one of those p-47s dive right ahead of you

340
00:28:50,401 --> 00:28:56,880
and you'd hear a big bomb boom and you knew rest assured when you got up there there was going to

341
00:28:56,881 --> 00:29:04,960
be a tank laying on the side there the allied armies with the third armored division often in

342
00:29:04,961 --> 00:29:13,280
the van used combined arms to great advantage as they swept across northern france they had become

343
00:29:13,281 --> 00:29:17,840
a veteran fighting team, well-versed in the art of armored warfare.

344
00:29:19,480 --> 00:29:23,020
The hardest thing I had to do was to shoot somebody.

345
00:29:23,460 --> 00:29:27,860
You know, that's just entirely foreign to the way you've been raised, is to shoot and

346
00:29:27,861 --> 00:29:28,561
kill somebody.

347
00:29:29,320 --> 00:29:33,520
And so the first few times you step on that trigger, knowing that you're really trying

348
00:29:33,521 --> 00:29:35,080
to kill somebody, it was really difficult.

349
00:29:35,900 --> 00:29:42,300
But after you've been in combat for a while, you see other men killed, you begin to get

350
00:29:42,301 --> 00:29:45,620
the feeling it's going to be me or them, and if it's got to be that way, it's going to

351
00:29:45,621 --> 00:29:46,600
be them and not me.

352
00:29:49,420 --> 00:29:54,580
For all its problems, the Sherman had one beloved asset, reliability.

353
00:29:55,540 --> 00:30:00,400
The men who fought the tank appreciated its simple, dependable, rugged design.

354
00:30:01,680 --> 00:30:03,760
And so did the men who fixed them.

355
00:30:06,340 --> 00:30:11,340
A well-trained mechanic was worth his weight in gold to a mechanized army on the move.

356
00:30:12,300 --> 00:30:18,060
Repairs were made as close to the front as possible to help maintain the momentum of the offensive.

357
00:30:20,920 --> 00:30:28,640
The ordnance units in Europe were pushed farther forward than anyone thought was possible.

358
00:30:29,640 --> 00:30:33,820
And these units were pushed right up behind the front lines.

359
00:30:34,420 --> 00:30:37,880
And sometimes with shifting front lines, they were the front lines.

360
00:30:37,881 --> 00:30:44,700
Tanks knocked out in combat were picked up by giant recovery trucks

361
00:30:44,701 --> 00:30:50,940
and brought back to a secure rear area called a vehicle collection point, or VCP.

362
00:30:53,320 --> 00:30:57,400
The first step in repairing a tank often proved to be the most difficult,

363
00:30:58,640 --> 00:31:01,400
removing the mangled bodies of the dead crewmen.

364
00:31:02,100 --> 00:31:03,920
These tanks were coming to that VCP.

365
00:31:04,080 --> 00:31:05,620
They were just shot all to hell.

366
00:31:05,621 --> 00:31:10,740
I mean, you couldn't believe it, and the bodies are still in there, and we had to get the bodies out.

367
00:31:11,200 --> 00:31:15,860
If a guy gets hit in the head, the head just explodes, the brains and everything just go over the tank.

368
00:31:15,900 --> 00:31:16,600
It's horrible.

369
00:31:17,280 --> 00:31:21,120
The brains get all down the turret racing and the electrical wiring and the harness,

370
00:31:21,260 --> 00:31:24,060
and you just have to rip all that stuff out and clean it out thoroughly.

371
00:31:25,100 --> 00:31:29,000
Then after they do that, they replace the harness, and they paint the tank all over inside.

372
00:31:29,820 --> 00:31:34,700
And even after you paint it, there's that still faint stench of death that comes through that paint.

373
00:31:34,701 --> 00:31:38,879
you're not sure, but it's always there.

374
00:31:38,880 --> 00:31:43,700
Shot up tanks could be fixed, but experienced

375
00:31:43,701 --> 00:31:45,866
crewmen were lost for good.

376
00:31:45,867 --> 00:31:50,760
Once in combat, it became virtually impossible to get trained

377
00:31:50,761 --> 00:31:52,319
replacement crews.

378
00:31:52,320 --> 00:31:58,120
New crewmen were trained in a matter of days, sometimes not at all.

379
00:31:58,121 --> 00:32:01,960
I remember once getting a replacement.

380
00:32:03,080 --> 00:32:06,560
I asked the guy his name and got that.

381
00:32:06,700 --> 00:32:09,800
And I said, what kind of training did you get?

382
00:32:10,040 --> 00:32:10,880
And he says, infantry.

383
00:32:11,120 --> 00:32:12,620
I said, did you go to Fort Knox?

384
00:32:12,700 --> 00:32:13,400
No.

385
00:32:14,320 --> 00:32:16,340
I says, have you been in a tank before?

386
00:32:16,420 --> 00:32:17,140
And he said, no.

387
00:32:18,060 --> 00:32:19,540
I says, oh, my God.

388
00:32:19,600 --> 00:32:20,720
I said, what are you doing here?

389
00:32:21,220 --> 00:32:24,820
Well, we were lining up to move out.

390
00:32:24,821 --> 00:32:29,460
and so I put the kid in the assistant driver's seat.

391
00:32:30,460 --> 00:32:33,620
The next thing you know, a few shells came in.

392
00:32:34,040 --> 00:32:36,740
This kid gets hit, but not bad.

393
00:32:37,080 --> 00:32:40,900
In fact, I don't even know how bad it was.

394
00:32:41,340 --> 00:32:44,020
And he took off on me because we hadn't gone far.

395
00:32:44,660 --> 00:32:45,600
Took off running.

396
00:32:46,560 --> 00:32:48,780
I still, to this day, don't know his name.

397
00:32:49,620 --> 00:32:54,180
I never saw him again, and I never found out what happened.

398
00:32:54,181 --> 00:32:55,680
But that was war.

399
00:32:58,320 --> 00:33:00,420
Now, later on, we began to lose so many men

400
00:33:00,421 --> 00:33:02,360
that we had to operate with a three-man crew.

401
00:33:02,460 --> 00:33:06,540
We had a driver and a tank commander and a gunner.

402
00:33:07,000 --> 00:33:10,800
So then they sent down two truckloads of raw recruit infantry.

403
00:33:11,400 --> 00:33:13,000
They brought them down, we lined them all up,

404
00:33:13,900 --> 00:33:17,220
said, how many of you guys have ever operated a tank?

405
00:33:17,620 --> 00:33:18,900
None of them have been in a tank.

406
00:33:19,020 --> 00:33:21,440
Some of them have never seen a tank before, not close up.

407
00:33:22,060 --> 00:33:23,040
So we got them out there,

408
00:33:23,041 --> 00:33:24,580
So we took them and divided them into crews.

409
00:33:25,420 --> 00:33:29,040
And so we took a tank driver and two men, put them in a tank.

410
00:33:29,820 --> 00:33:31,840
We let each man fire three rounds apiece.

411
00:33:32,020 --> 00:33:33,600
That's the only training they had on that gun.

412
00:33:33,700 --> 00:33:35,080
That's all they had the first time.

413
00:33:36,100 --> 00:33:38,200
And about 5 o'clock that night, we released them.

414
00:33:38,580 --> 00:33:41,380
And going out about 8 o'clock that night, three hours later,

415
00:33:41,480 --> 00:33:44,320
out of that 17 tanks, 15 were knocked out alongside the road.

416
00:33:44,740 --> 00:33:47,020
And I don't know whether any of those young guys survived or not.

417
00:33:47,760 --> 00:33:49,540
They had no idea what they were getting into.

418
00:33:49,960 --> 00:33:51,860
And I thought, how in the hell can you send these kids?

419
00:33:51,861 --> 00:33:53,660
but there was nobody else to send. That was it.

420
00:33:58,000 --> 00:34:02,580
By the end of August, the 3rd Armored Division had moved out of northern France

421
00:34:02,581 --> 00:34:07,260
and through the cities of Mont and Liège, Belgium, on their way to Germany.

422
00:34:09,280 --> 00:34:12,340
Major General Maurice Rose, 3rd Armored Commander,

423
00:34:12,840 --> 00:34:16,520
had pushed his division farther and faster than anyone thought possible.

424
00:34:16,521 --> 00:34:23,420
The autumn rains had turned the European countryside into a quagmire.

425
00:34:24,920 --> 00:34:29,980
The tracks had to be fitted with special extended end connectors called duck bills

426
00:34:29,981 --> 00:34:32,880
to help the tanks move through the mud.

427
00:34:36,080 --> 00:34:40,040
On September 12th, a reconnaissance platoon of the 3rd Armored

428
00:34:40,041 --> 00:34:44,780
had crossed from Belgium into Germany and occupied a small border town.

429
00:34:46,520 --> 00:34:53,960
For the first time since the Napoleonic Wars, a German town had fallen to an invading army.

430
00:34:56,520 --> 00:35:03,160
The soldiers of the 3rd Armored, the 2nd Armored, the 4th Infantry, and a host of other divisions

431
00:35:03,161 --> 00:35:06,200
wasted no time in assaulting the Siegfried Line,

432
00:35:06,800 --> 00:35:10,260
the complex network of German bunkers and defensive fortifications.

433
00:35:10,261 --> 00:35:23,180
From now on, Hitler's soldiers would be defending their home turf, and for them, no sacrifice would be too great to blunt the spearhead of the armored warriors.

434
00:35:25,140 --> 00:35:25,840
The U.S.

435
00:35:25,841 --> 00:35:36,960
Army Ordnance Department, in charge of the design and maintenance of all weapons, grew from 334 officers in 1940 to 24,000 by the time of the Normandy invasion.

436
00:35:37,780 --> 00:35:39,420
Tank crews will return in a moment.

437
00:35:40,260 --> 00:35:49,100
The American armored and infantry divisions cleared a path through the vaunted Siegfried Line by the fall of 1944.

438
00:35:51,680 --> 00:35:57,600
Even though German resistance was stiff, it looked like the Allies could end the war by Christmas.

439
00:35:57,601 --> 00:36:09,720
but on December 16th the German army launched a massive counter-attack and drove a deep wedge

440
00:36:09,721 --> 00:36:13,920
between the Allied forces they hit with everything they had

441
00:36:20,520 --> 00:36:26,460
the American Air Force couldn't fly in the lousy weather and the tankers found themselves fighting

442
00:36:26,461 --> 00:36:27,360
for their lives.

443
00:36:28,220 --> 00:36:30,760
You know, you keep wondering how much longer it can go on

444
00:36:30,761 --> 00:36:34,260
or am I going to get it tomorrow or, you know, what's going to happen?

445
00:36:37,340 --> 00:36:41,160
You get to where it's a survival of the fittest

446
00:36:41,161 --> 00:36:42,900
and if you're lucky, you make it.

447
00:36:45,320 --> 00:36:49,320
I think there was a time when I felt that I could have died

448
00:36:49,321 --> 00:36:50,820
and that would have been all right.

449
00:36:51,080 --> 00:36:54,500
I wrote to my folks once when I thought maybe that time was coming,

450
00:36:54,501 --> 00:37:05,840
But I did tell them that they should know that if I am killed, that I got more Germans or more Jerry's than would ever even up by getting me.

451
00:37:12,100 --> 00:37:20,640
The Ardennes Offensive, the so-called Battle of the Bulge, was without a doubt the armored warrior's most desperate and miserable fight.

452
00:37:20,641 --> 00:37:25,220
He would lose a tank and see his crew shot all to hell,

453
00:37:25,221 --> 00:37:28,820
and then get back in a tank and still fight and lose that one and lose another, and he kept going.

454
00:37:29,320 --> 00:37:33,720
We've had somebody who lost three and four and five tanks and still kept fighting.

455
00:37:34,560 --> 00:37:35,740
Those men were courageous.

456
00:37:40,460 --> 00:37:41,980
The bravest men I ever saw.

457
00:37:45,940 --> 00:37:47,980
They were determined to hold their ground.

458
00:37:47,981 --> 00:37:50,360
and with the return of clear skies,

459
00:37:50,880 --> 00:37:53,940
they once again enjoyed the full support of American air power.

460
00:37:54,660 --> 00:37:58,260
The Allies attacked, and by February 1945,

461
00:37:59,060 --> 00:38:02,600
the 3rd Armored Division had finally reoccupied the area

462
00:38:02,601 --> 00:38:06,820
around the German towns of Aachen, Stolberg, and Mausbach,

463
00:38:07,080 --> 00:38:09,140
the region it held before the bulge.

464
00:38:13,940 --> 00:38:17,560
In mid-February, the division tankers received

465
00:38:17,561 --> 00:38:21,940
in answer to their prayers, the Army's first heavy tank.

466
00:38:23,300 --> 00:38:27,740
Called the M26 Pershing, it was a radical new design,

467
00:38:28,420 --> 00:38:31,200
conceived from the very start as a tank killer.

468
00:38:33,400 --> 00:38:36,620
Built by the Detroit and Grand Blanc tank arsenals,

469
00:38:37,380 --> 00:38:40,840
it was armed with an incredibly powerful 90-millimeter gun.

470
00:38:42,040 --> 00:38:46,600
It had a very low silhouette, with armor up to 4 1⁄2 inches thick

471
00:38:46,601 --> 00:38:49,380
and a torsion bar suspension system

472
00:38:49,381 --> 00:38:53,360
reminiscent of what Walter Christie had tried to get the Army to adopt

473
00:38:53,361 --> 00:38:55,000
more than a decade earlier.

474
00:38:56,440 --> 00:39:00,400
The M-26 more than made up for the deficiencies of the Sherman.

475
00:39:05,300 --> 00:39:06,940
By the end of February,

476
00:39:07,660 --> 00:39:10,780
200 Pershings had been issued to the various armored units.

477
00:39:11,360 --> 00:39:13,640
The 3rd Armored Division got 10

478
00:39:13,641 --> 00:39:16,480
and wasted no time in putting them to use.

479
00:39:16,600 --> 00:39:26,920
Within a week, the new heavy tanks were leading the division to its next objective in Germany,

480
00:39:27,680 --> 00:39:30,040
the once grand city of Cologne.

481
00:39:33,200 --> 00:39:37,640
Clarence Smoyer was a gunner leading the way in one of the new Pershings.

482
00:39:38,000 --> 00:39:45,540
We finally moved ahead more, and I remember one of the lieutenants got on the phone,

483
00:39:45,541 --> 00:39:48,380
and he said, gentlemen, I give you Cologne.

484
00:39:48,920 --> 00:39:50,620
Let's knock the hell out of it.

485
00:39:57,820 --> 00:40:01,560
A Signal Corps photographer, Tech Sergeant Jim Bates,

486
00:40:02,140 --> 00:40:05,060
tagged along with the division as it rolled into Cologne.

487
00:40:06,020 --> 00:40:09,340
His film footage captured the extremes of the armored war,

488
00:40:10,240 --> 00:40:12,340
the vulnerability of the Sherman,

489
00:40:12,820 --> 00:40:14,460
and the power of the new Pershing.

490
00:40:15,540 --> 00:40:20,780
His images are some of the most dramatic and gut-wrenching of the entire war.

491
00:40:25,600 --> 00:40:28,660
In this shot, a Sherman has just been hit.

492
00:40:29,340 --> 00:40:34,940
A crewman, his foot severed in the attack, falls and bleeds to death.

493
00:40:34,941 --> 00:40:46,480
the Pershing crew spots the enemy tank a mark 5 Panther moving behind a building

494
00:40:46,481 --> 00:40:52,380
the cameraman moves ahead and continues filming as the m26 stalks its prey

495
00:40:52,381 --> 00:41:01,680
so we've started to fire into that building which he was a side of I thought maybe if we fire

496
00:41:01,681 --> 00:41:05,660
armor-piercing shells, they would go through the building and maybe still get him.

497
00:41:06,780 --> 00:41:09,840
But evidently, he just kept

498
00:41:09,841 --> 00:41:12,800
on backing up and went all the way down to the cathedral.

499
00:41:15,560 --> 00:41:17,640
The German tank moves into the

500
00:41:17,641 --> 00:41:21,500
plaza of the Grand Cathedral. The Pershing pursues.

501
00:41:22,740 --> 00:41:25,260
Smoyer spots his target and opens fire.

502
00:41:26,480 --> 00:41:29,940
I fired first one, first shell on the run.

503
00:41:29,941 --> 00:41:36,180
hit him, and then we stop and fire in another one.

504
00:41:40,020 --> 00:41:41,980
The German driver is killed.

505
00:41:42,860 --> 00:41:44,580
The commander and the gunner bail out.

506
00:41:50,040 --> 00:41:51,960
Smoyer fires a third time.

507
00:41:53,900 --> 00:41:56,600
The concussion from his gun rocks the camera.

508
00:41:56,601 --> 00:41:59,960
ammunition set off by the shell

509
00:41:59,961 --> 00:42:02,660
turns the panther into a funeral pyre

510
00:42:02,661 --> 00:42:05,820
it sat and burned

511
00:42:05,821 --> 00:42:07,620
for three days

512
00:42:07,621 --> 00:42:10,080
I didn't go back and look

513
00:42:10,081 --> 00:42:12,180
in that afterward because I knew some of them

514
00:42:12,181 --> 00:42:13,120
died in there

515
00:42:13,121 --> 00:42:15,420
I spent a lot of time

516
00:42:15,421 --> 00:42:17,880
wondering if any of them

517
00:42:17,881 --> 00:42:18,581
survived

518
00:42:18,582 --> 00:42:20,360
it still hurts

519
00:42:20,361 --> 00:42:23,480
even though they were the enemy

520
00:42:23,481 --> 00:42:26,000
they were still

521
00:42:26,001 --> 00:42:31,480
humans.

522
00:42:31,481 --> 00:42:38,220
In two months, Germany would surrender and the war in Europe would be over.

523
00:42:38,221 --> 00:42:43,320
Because of its zeal to lead the way, the 3rd Armored Division was officially dubbed the

524
00:42:43,321 --> 00:42:45,320
Spearhead Division.

525
00:42:45,321 --> 00:42:49,400
But being the vanguard came with a price.

526
00:42:49,401 --> 00:42:57,300
The third armored lost 648 of its tanks in combat, and another 700 were damaged in action.

527
00:43:00,500 --> 00:43:04,900
It also suffered more battle casualties than any other armored division.

528
00:43:06,320 --> 00:43:11,000
10,371 men killed, wounded, or missing.

529
00:43:12,420 --> 00:43:15,680
Including its commander, Major General Maurice Rose.

530
00:43:16,020 --> 00:43:18,860
He was killed in action near Paderborn, Germany.

531
00:43:19,400 --> 00:43:21,360
just weeks after the assault on Cologne.

532
00:43:26,180 --> 00:43:29,880
During the war, Harley Swenson escaped from three tanks,

533
00:43:30,620 --> 00:43:32,140
and so did Walter Stitt.

534
00:43:35,660 --> 00:43:37,780
Bert Close bailed out of four,

535
00:43:40,180 --> 00:43:44,340
and Clement Alessandro had survived the loss of five Shermans.

536
00:43:44,341 --> 00:43:50,120
And thanks to his job of having to account for all the battle-damaged tanks,

537
00:43:50,940 --> 00:43:56,940
Belton Cooper holds the distinction of seeing more destroyed Shermans than any man alive.

538
00:43:58,780 --> 00:44:02,220
I said, what a terrible thing war does to a young man.

539
00:44:03,520 --> 00:44:05,700
It takes him to the prime of his life.

540
00:44:07,720 --> 00:44:10,280
It demeans him. It humiliates him.

541
00:44:10,281 --> 00:44:14,120
it destroys the last vestiges of human dignity

542
00:44:14,121 --> 00:44:16,580
you have no dignity in the army or the war at all

543
00:44:16,581 --> 00:44:21,060
and I said sometimes it kills him or horribly maims him

544
00:44:21,061 --> 00:44:25,260
and those of us that survive are never quite the same

